==Russian==

===Etymology===
{{root|ru|ine-pro|*(s)mel-}}
{{inh+|ru|orv|малъ}}, from {{inh|ru|sla-pro|*malъ}}, from {{der|ru|ine-pro|*(s)mal-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*(s)mel-}}, whence English {{m|en|small}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ru-IPA|ма́лый}}
* {{audio|ru|Ru-малый.ogg}}

===Adjective===
{{ru-adj|ма́лый|ме́ньше|мале́йший,ме́ньший|adv=ма́ло,пома́лу,ма́ло-пома́лу|absn=+|dim=ма́ленький,малю́сенький|intens=ма́ленький,малю́сенький,малёхонький,малёшенький|attenu=малова́тый}}

# [[small]], [[little]]
# [[short]]

====Usage notes====
* The short form should not be conflated with the adjective {{m|ru|мал||too small}}, which has [[lexicalize]]d as a short-form-only adjective in its own right.
* Special short forms are found in the following expressions:
** {{uxi|ru|от '''ма́ла''' до вели́ка|t=[[all ages]]|lit=from small to big}}
**: {{m|ru|ма́ла}} and {{m|ru|вели́ка}} are old short genitives.
** {{uxi|ru|ку́ча '''мала́'''|t=pile of people; disorderly heap|lit=small pile}}
**: {{m|ru|мала́|g=f}} is an old short attributive adjective.
** {{uxi|ru|'''мал''' '''мала́''' ме́ньше|t=one  smaller than the next|lit=small small smaller}}
**: {{m|ru|мал|g=m}} and {{m|ru|мала́|g=f}} are old short attributive adjectives.

